# Clinical write authority and prescription integrity (rule 228)

**Status:** scope of `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8C` (2026-11-07). Sprint record:
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-8c.md`. Owner decisions of the Program 8 owner-decision checkpoint
(`DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8-OWNER-OPERATIONAL-CLOSURE-1`, 2026-11-07): D3 Group 1 "FIX NOW", D3-NEW-4 "FIX NOW",
D3 Group 2 "FIX NOW (lock after completion)", signature "named doctor signs only".
Category under the Foundation-First Sprint Lock: security fix / clinical correctness.

This rule EXTENDS rules 138, 226 and 227. Where they differ, this rule wins:

- rule 226 §1 and rule 227 §3 said prescriptions carry "no room rule" (OD-7-2 open) — §1 below adds it;
- rule 227 §1.3 said every account that is not a context-bound non-clinical one keeps the RME-wide document read —
  §5 below narrows that to an allowlist;
- rule 138's room scope bound the visit, the RME page and the odontogram — §1 and §2 extend it to prescriptions and
  to the live-encounter write authority. Its §17 boundary (history is never room-hidden) is unchanged.

## 1. Prescriptions follow the doctor room scope (OD-7-2, R8A-1)

1. `RmePrescriptionPolicy` (`view`, `print`, `viewForVisit`, `create`, `update`) applies the doctor-patient scope and
   then `DoctorRoomScopeService::authorizeActiveVisitRoom()`: a doctor never opens or writes the prescription of an
   ACTIVE visit (registered / waiting / in_progress) of another treatment room.
2. `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::permits()` re-asserts it (`deniesActiveVisitOutsideRoom()`), so a caller that skips
   the route and the policy is refused the same way (CG-06).
3. Post-examination and historical visits (cashier_pending, completed, cancelled) are never room-restricted, and an
   account that is not doctor-scoped (Supervisor RME, Owner, Super Admin) is unaffected.
4. Not covered (recorded so this rule is not read as total): the clinic-oversight print surface (route
   `oversight.rme.visits.prescription.print`, `OversightClinicalRecordController`) renders the same document under its
   own `clinic-oversight.*` gate abilities, outside `RmePrescriptionPolicy`, so neither this room scope nor the
   §5 read allowlist applies to it. It is read-only, audited and separately governed (independent review F7).

## 2. The live-encounter write authority is room scoped (OD-5-7)

1. `RmeVisitConsentService::actorMayWorkOnEncounter()` — the single predicate behind current-RME writes, handwriting,
   odontogram charting and performed treatments — refuses a doctor whose live room is not the encounter's room. The
   current-RME write is keyed on the patient's canonical record book, so it never reached the visit's room guard.
2. A doctor with no live room context is refused on an ACTIVE encounter (fail closed), as rule 138 already refuses the
   visit page itself.

## 3. Starting an examination is a clinical act (OD-5-2)

1. `ClinicVisitPolicy::startExamination()` is the clinical examination authority (`complete_rme_examination` + working
   branch + doctor-patient scope — the same as `completeExamination()`). The controller authorizes it for
   `in_progress`, and `ClinicVisitService::transitionStatus()` re-asserts it for every caller.
2. The front desk keeps CANCEL (a no-show): `cancelled` is not gated by it.
3. The visit page offers "Mulai Pemeriksaan" only to an account that holds the ability; hiding it is not the boundary.

## 4. A finished examination keeps its doctor (OD-5-3)

`ClinicVisitService::assignRoom()` on a `cashier_pending` visit moves only the room; `doctor_id` and the
patient-doctor assignment stay with the doctor who examined. Pre-examination placement still resolves the online
doctor of the room exactly as before. Terminal visits stay refused.

## 5. Role allowlists, not denylists (D3-NEW-1, D3-NEW-4)

1. RME / odontogram / performed-treatment authoring requires a role in `FrontOfficeRole::CLINICAL_ROLES` (the same list
   prescriptions use, rule 227 §3) on top of `manage_clinic_visits`. A role created at runtime and given
   `manage_clinic_visits` (production holds one, `Tester RME`, no holders) does not inherit authoring.
2. Cross-branch clinical-document READ is `RmeWorkingBranchScope::CROSS_BRANCH_DOCUMENT_READ_ROLES` (clinical roles +
   Owner). The context-bound front desk and cashier keep their working branch (rule 227 §1.2); any other account reads
   NO clinical document until it is classified. A new role that must read across branches is added on purpose.
3. Every seeded role that holds a clinic-visit permission is classified — pinned by
   `RmeClinicalDocumentReadScopeAllowlistTest` A-05.

## 6. Not changed by §1–§5

No schema, migration, permission, role, route or package change. No flag, enforcement scope, branch lock, queue
worker, scheduler or KTP timer change. The Program 8B foundations (rule 227 §1–§4) are unchanged except where this
rule narrows them.

## 7. Critical-gate coverage (CI-8C)

`RmeClinicalWriteAuthorityTest` is selected by the new `RmeClinicalWriteAuthority` token in BOTH critical-gate filter
variants; `RmeClinicalDocumentReadScopeAllowlistTest` is selected by the existing `RmeClinicalDocumentReadScope`
token. Both are declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 8. A closed visit's prescription is read-only (OD-8A-1)

Owner decision, taken after the reviewer combined OD-8A-1 and OD-8A-2 into one HIGH: **lock after completion**.

1. `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::documentLocked()` is the one definition: a prescription whose visit is
   `completed` or `cancelled` is read-only for every account, Super Admin included. `cashier_pending` is not closed —
   the doctor may still correct it before it is paid.
2. `RmePrescriptionPolicy::create()` / `update()` refuse it (403 with the reason), and `RmePrescriptionService`
   re-decides it on the visit row it holds FOR NO KEY UPDATE (`update()` now takes that lock first, as `create()`
   always did — rule 223 §1). Super Admin's `Gate::before` skips the policy; the service still refuses (CG-06).
   The whole authoring authority (`assertPermits()`: branch, doctor-patient scope, room) is re-decided on that locked
   row as well (AIR-RMC-38; independent review F3), so a room move committed between the pre-lock check and the lock
   is not missed.
3. Reading, printing and sending a closed prescription are unchanged. Printing still stamps `printed_at` — print
   bookkeeping, not an amendment of the clinical content.

## 9. Every prescription write is audited (OD-8A-2)

One `sys_audit_logs` row per create (`RME_PRESCRIPTION_CREATED`) and per update (`RME_PRESCRIPTION_UPDATED`),
`entity_type = trx_rme_prescriptions`, written in the SAME transaction as the write — a rolled-back write leaves no
row. The payload is ids, the locked visit's status, the actor and the NAMES of the changed fields (canvas columns
without `_path`): never a value, never patient data, never a storage key. A refused write writes nothing.

## 10. Canvas payloads are bounded (P8R-S5)

1. The FormRequests cap the R/ payload at `PrescriptionCanvasDecoder::MAX_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` (3,500,000) and the
   signature at `MAX_SIGNATURE_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` (1,000,000). The application's own fixed canvases (900×480 and 400×140)
   stay far below both.
2. The decoder applies `MAX_PAYLOAD_LENGTH` (or a tighter per-field `maxLength` a caller passes — the prescription
   service passes the signature limit; a caller can only tighten it) again before decoding, requires a real IHDR as
   the first chunk, refuses a declared size above `MAX_DIMENSION` (2048) per side or `MAX_PIXELS` (1,048,576, about
   2.4x the largest canvas drawn — independent review F4, F5), and inflates the image data bounded by the declared
   size — a small, highly compressed payload cannot ask for a huge decode.
3. The decoder is SHARED with `RmeVisitConsentService` (consent signatures, 700×220 canvases): the IHDR, dimension,
   pixel and inflate bounds and the generic 3,500,000 payload bound apply to consent signatures too. The tighter
   1,000,000 signature payload limit does NOT yet apply to them (no request `max`, no `maxLength` passed) — open LOW,
   §15.

## 11. The consent signature is not cached (OD-6-2)

`RmeVisitConsentController::signature()` streams with `Cache-Control: private, no-store, max-age=0`, as the
prescription canvases already do.

## 12. Only the prescription's own doctor signs (D3-NEW-2, OD-5-1)

1. `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::maySign()` — the account linked to the prescription's own `doctor_id` through
   `DoctorIdentityResolver` (the one identity link) is the only one that may save the doctor-signature canvas.
   Identity, not role: a Super Admin, Owner or Supervisor RME that is not that doctor is refused, and `Gate::before`
   has nothing to bypass.
2. Another clinician keeps content authoring (OD-5-1 kept): on create the signature is optional for them and the
   prescription is stored unsigned; a submitted signature is refused before either canvas is decoded or written, and
   on update the stored signature is never replaced by another account. The named doctor signs on create as before,
   or adds the signature later with an update.
3. The form offers the signature canvas only to that doctor (`canSign` from the controller — never decided in Blade);
   anyone else sees the stored signature read-only or "Belum ditandatangani". Hiding it is not the boundary.
4. An unsigned prescription prints without the signature image, under the doctor's name (unchanged print template).

## 13. Removed: the uncalled RME-wide `MedicalRecordService::paginate()` (D3-NEW-6)

It listed medical records across every RME branch with only the doctor scope applied and had no caller. It is gone,
with its now-unused dependency. The repository's `paginateForBranches()` stays: it takes the branch set explicitly,
so no caller can reach the estate by omission.

## 14. Critical-gate coverage (PR2)

`RmePrescriptionIntegrityTest` is selected by the existing `RmePrescription` token in BOTH critical-gate variants and
declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

